# Toolang Syntax Reference
#
# Status
# - This file describes the intended direction of Toolang.
# - It is written as a `.too` source file so the syntax stays concrete.
# - The bootstrap runtime may implement only a subset of the forms below.
#
# Design goals
# - readable by humans
# - editable by agents
# - structured enough for deterministic runtime behavior
# - simple enough to stay friendly outside traditional software engineering
#
# Core language idea
# - a `.too` file describes one agent
# - `thunk` declares an executable agent entrypoint
# - capabilities and data declarations provide context for that entrypoint
# - the runtime decides how a thunk is invoked from CLI, API, chat, or batch
#
# Lexical conventions
# - `# ...` starts a comment
# - top-level forms begin at column 1
# - indented lines belong to the preceding `thunk` body
# - fenced blocks use ```lang ... ```
# - `struct` names use `PascalCase`
# - `stash`, `prompt`, `service`, `psyche`, and named `thunk` names use `kebab-case`
# - external references use `namespace/name`


# Top-level forms
#
# - import statement:
#   `use <kind> <reference>`
#
# - struct declaration:
#   `struct <Name>: ```lang`
#
# - stash declaration:
#   `stash <name>: ```python`
#
# - inline capability declaration:
#   `service <name>: ```md`
#   `prompt <name>(...): ```md`
#   `psyche <name>: ```md`
#
# - entrypoint declaration:
#   `thunk [name] [([user])] [=> Struct]:`


# Runtime model
#
# Toolang separates language-level declarations from runtime-level execution:
#
# - `tools`
#   the callable tool list passed into a model call
#
# - `memory`
#   a persistent runtime-managed memory subsystem
#   memory is not declared directly in source files
#   memory may expose tools such as `memory.write` or `memory.forget`
#
# - `stash`
#   run-scoped shared working data
#   stash is visible across multiple steps in a single agent run
#   stash is not persistent by default
#
# - `thunk` body
#   contributes to system-side instruction assembly
#
# - `thunk(user)`
#   contributes the user-side message payload
#
# A typical model call is assembled as:
# - system message:
#   thunk body, visible capabilities, runtime policy, memory search results, and
#   other runtime context
# - user message:
#   the thunk input, optionally after prompt-template expansion


# Capability kinds
#
# - `skill`: reusable capability bundle
# - `service`: external service or MCP endpoint
# - `prompt`: reusable user-message template
# - `psyche`: reusable behavioral or persona fragment


# 1. Import Statements
#
# `use` imports a capability defined outside the current file.
# Imported capabilities are visible to thunks unless restricted by directives.

use skill briceyan/pdf-processing
use skill anthropic/create-skill

use service github/github
use service briceyan/linear

use prompt briceyan/tocn

use psyche briceyan/senior-rust-engineer
use psyche briceyan/project-manager


# 2. Struct Declarations
#
# `struct` defines reusable structured data.
# The bootstrap runtime is expected to treat `json` as the default structured
# shape language for model output contracts.

struct WorkspaceSummary: ```json
{
  "title": "string",
  "summary": "string",
  "items": ["string"]
}
```


# 3. Stash Declarations
#
# `stash` defines run-scoped shared working data.
# The first stash declaration language is `python`.
# Its contents may look like a dataclass plus methods.
# The runtime is responsible for interpreting stash updates and applying state
# changes between steps.
#
# Future versions may restrict the allowed Python subset or support additional
# declaration languages.

stash todo-list: ```python
items: list[str] = []
selected: str | None = None

def add(item: str) -> None:
    items.append(item)

def remove(item: str) -> None:
    items.remove(item)

def clear() -> None:
    items.clear()
```


# 4. Service Declarations
#
# `service` defines an inline service capability.
# Markdown is the recommended body format.
# Service metadata lives in front matter inside the Markdown block.
#
# Required front matter fields:
# - `transport`
# - `target`
#
# Recommended:
# - `description`
#
# The front matter format is a service-level convention, not a separate Toolang
# top-level syntax form.

service github: ```md
---
transport: http
target: https://mcp.github.com/mcp
description: GitHub MCP server
---

Use this service when the agent needs to read or modify GitHub resources.
```

service linear: ```md
---
transport: stdio
target: npx -y mcp-remote xxxx
description: Linear MCP server
---

Use this service when the agent needs to read or modify Linear issues.
```


# 5. Prompt Template Declarations
#
# `prompt` defines a user-message template.
# Prompt parameters are always strings, so parameter types are omitted.
# `?` marks an optional parameter.
#
# Template variables use `{{name}}`.
# `{{input}}` is an implicit variable bound to the message body that follows the
# slash-command line.
#
# Prompt expansion rules:
# - only the first line of the user message may trigger a prompt
# - each user message may invoke at most one `/template-name`
# - prompt expansion does not nest
# - expansion happens before the model call
#
# Example user message:
# `/summarize style=brief audience="team leads"`
#
# Followed by a blank line and the free-form body text that becomes `{{input}}`.

prompt summarize(style, audience?): ```md
Summarize the user's request in a {{style}} style.
Target audience: {{audience}}

{{input}}
```

prompt reply-in-chinese: ```md
Answer in Chinese.
```


# 6. Psyche Declarations
#
# `psyche` defines a reusable behavioral fragment that may contribute to the
# system-side instruction set assembled by the runtime.

psyche senior-rust-engineer: ```md
Think like a pragmatic senior Rust engineer.
```


# 7. Entrypoint Declarations
#
# `thunk` is the executable entrypoint form of the language.
# A thunk is intentionally narrower than a general function.
#
# Header forms:
# - `thunk:`
# - `thunk name:`
# - `thunk => Struct:`
# - `thunk name => Struct:`
# - `thunk(user):`
# - `thunk name(user):`
# - `thunk(user) => Struct:`
# - `thunk name(user) => Struct:`
#
# Input rules:
# - a thunk accepts either no input or exactly one input
# - if present, that input represents the user message
# - thunk input types are not declared; the input is semantically a user message
#
# Output rules:
# - if present, the output references a `struct`
# - output is a response contract, not a second input channel
#
# Body rules:
# - optional directive phase first
# - blank line separator
# - natural-language agent instruction body
#
# Directive intent:
# directives constrain visibility or selection of runtime-provided capabilities.
# They do not define tools directly.
#
# Collection subjects:
# - `skills`
# - `services`
# - `tools`
# - `thunks`
#
# Collection operators:
# - `subject -`
#   clear the visible set
# - `subject = a, b, c`
#   replace the visible set with the listed items
# - `subject - a, b, c`
#   remove the listed items from the visible set
#
# Model selection:
# - `model = default`
#   use the runtime default model
# - omitting `model` is equivalent to `model = default`
# - `model = gpt-5.3`
#   use a single explicit model
# - `model = gpt-5.3, gpt-5.3-mini, default`
#   try the listed models in order, then fall back to the runtime default
#
# Memory tools may appear inside tool directives, for example:
# - `memory.write`
# - `memory.save`
# - `memory.forget`

thunk summarize-workspace => WorkspaceSummary:
    skills   = briceyan/pdf-processing, anthropic/create-skill
    services - linear
    tools    - memory.write, memory.forget
    thunks   -
    model    = gpt-5.3, gpt-5.3-mini, default

    Read the available context.
    Perform the task.
    Return a response matching WorkspaceSummary.

thunk answer(user) => WorkspaceSummary:
    tools    - memory.forget
    model    = gpt-5.3

    Treat the thunk body as system-side instruction.
    Treat the thunk input as the user message.
    If the user message starts with a prompt invocation,
    expand it before the model call.
    Return a response matching WorkspaceSummary.


# 8. Default Entrypoint
#
# If no thunk name is specified, the thunk is the default entrypoint.
# If no output is specified, the runtime decides the output mode.
# If no directives are specified, all visible capabilities are available.

thunk:
    Act as the default agent entrypoint.
    Use the declared capabilities unless restricted by runtime policy.


# 9. Remaining Design Work
#
# The core direction is set, but several areas still need additional design:
#
# - module and version declarations
# - capability aliasing
# - formal validation rules for `struct`
# - formal runtime rules for `stash` mutation instructions
# - package composition across multiple `.too` files
# - visibility and export rules between files
